A1. This comes after a swimmer's leg. ~ SHARK
A5. Dame Winter's boy friends. ~ CHAPS
A10. You can't say "whereas" without saying this first. ~ WHERE
A11. The 400. ~ ELITE
A13. The Charter Oak got its name from this. ~ CHARTER
A15. This is always getting framed. ~ DIPLOMA
A17. This is full of holes. ~ LACE
A18. The one thing labor leaders don't do. ~ LABOR
A20. A shield or defensive armor. ~ EGIS
A21. A bird with a very prominent tooth. ~ ELK
A22. A jinrikisha's motor. ~ JAP
A23. A pirate would teethe on this if there were a D in front of it. ~ IRK
A24. In your hat. ~ FELT
A26. Fed up. ~ SATED
A28. Abbreviation of southeast, seen twice. ~ SESE
A29. This is always underfoot. ~ EARTH
A31. Sugar does this to flies. ~ DRAWS
A33. Golfers and stockholders think wistfully of this. ~ PAR
A34. What hockey players fight over. ~ ICE
A36. On the up and up. ~ LEGAL
A38. What you do when you're asleep. ~ SLEEP
A41. Every villain should do this. ~ LEER
A43. Not part of a wallflower. ~ PETAL
A45. A Spanish cartwheel. ~ PESO
A47. A good way to get results. ~ ADD
A48. Face. ~ PAN
A49. This is best inhaled through the nose. ~ AIR
A50. After the bald was over. (pl.) ~ WIGS
A52. Peeved. ~ VEXED
A54. Perennial picnickers. ~ ANTS
A55. Among Alfonso's souvenirs. ~ SCEPTER
A57. Merely another form of racket. ~ RACQUET
A59. One who doesn't succeed at first. ~ TRIER
A60. Chinese military formations. ~ ROUTS
A61. Knifes. ~ STABS
A62. Knowledge (pl.). ~ VEDAS

D1. To marry. ~ SHACKLE
D2. Four-letter word meaning "at hand." ~ HERE
D3. Arthur Whoozis. ~ ART
D4. Something the movies couldn't get along without. ~ REEL
D6. The prodigal son. ~ HEIR
D7. Something to yodel over. ~ ALP
D8. This stacks up as a heap. ~ PILE
D9. Three, for a nickel. ~ STOGIES
D10. Jonah's yacht. ~ WHALE
D12. What Arab mothers think their sons will be. ~ EMIRS
D13. Just another crack. ~ CLEFT
D14. A prince of India. ~ RAJAH
D15. What the victim's drink was. ~ DOPED
D16. Like an old bachelor's wig. ~ ASKEW
D19. A fly-by-night. ~ BAT
D25. Fancy name for candle. ~ TAPER
D26. An upper berth in the subway. ~ STRAP
D27. Favorite instrument of the "painless" dentist. ~ DRILL
D28. New brooms do this. ~ SWEEP
D30. This is chewed at tea parties. ~ RAG
D32. Better than a king. ~ ACE
D35. These are the cat's. ~ CLAWS
D36. Firms keep these to amuse their C.P.A. ~ LEDGERS
D37. You'll find this in a colony. ~ LEPER
D38. Not so dumb. ~ SANER
D39. These are often stowed in trunks. ~ PEANUTS
D40. If U were the second letter, this would be "baloney." ~ WORST
D42. A law to the king's taste. ~ EDICT
D44. A favorite word in Congress. ~ TAX
D46. Plots. ~ SITES
D51. A horrid word. ~ SPIT
D52. A word of action. ~ VERB
D53. A chip on the shoulder. ~ DARE
D54. A drink on the house (Latin). ~ AQUA
D56. Just an old Chinese custom. ~ TEA
D58. If this fish talked, it would have a Yankee twang. ~ COD


